--TEST--
A variable, a property, an array element and a parameter all reach one object: a write through any of them is read through all, `==` answers identity across every pair, and a `clone` is outside the set — `rule:classes/two-copy-depths`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/objects-are-handles
// `rule:classes/two-copy-depths`: assigning an object, passing it or storing it
// never copies it, and `==` on two objects is identity. The section names four
// carriers — a variable, a property, an array element and a parameter — and this
// case asks the same question of each, counting the agreements rather than
// reading one line per carrier: a carrier that grew its own copy still prints
// plausibly on its own line and fails the count here.

class Cell {
    public int $value = 0;
}

class Holder {
    public function constructor(public Cell $cell) {}
}

class Probe {
    // The parameter is the one carrier that cannot be spelled at the call site.
    public static function read(Cell $cell): int {
        return $cell->value;
    }

    public static function same(Cell $left, Cell $right): bool {
        return $left == $right;
    }
}

var $cell = new Cell();
var $holder = new Holder($cell);
array<Cell> $row = [$cell];

// Two writes, neither through the variable the object was born in.
$holder->cell->value = 41;
$row[0]->value = $row[0]->value + 1;

// Every carrier reads the one object: four readings, all 42.
array<int> $readings = [$cell->value, $holder->cell->value, $row[0]->value, Probe::read($cell)];
int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($readings as int $reading) {
    if ($reading == 42) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}
echo "agreed=", $agreed, " of ", Core\Arr::count($readings), "\n";

// `==` is identity, so each carrier is equal to every carrier including itself.
array<Cell> $carriers = [$cell, $holder->cell, $row[0]];
int $identical = 0;
foreach ($carriers as Cell $left) {
    foreach ($carriers as Cell $right) {
        if (Probe::same($left, $right)) {
            $identical = $identical + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "identical=", $identical, " of 9\n";

// A second object carrying the same value is equal to none of them, which is
// what says the nine above were identity and not a comparison of properties.
var $twin = new Cell();
$twin->value = 42;
int $matched = 0;
foreach ($carriers as Cell $each) {
    if (Probe::same($each, $twin)) {
        $matched = $matched + 1;
    }
}
echo "matched=", $matched, " values equal=", ($twin->value == $cell->value) as string, "\n";

// `clone` is the one operation that leaves the set: the copy is a different
// object, and a write through it reaches no carrier.
var $copy = clone $cell;
$copy->value = 7;
int $reached = 0;
foreach ($carriers as Cell $each) {
    if ($each->value == 7 || Probe::same($each, $copy)) {
        $reached = $reached + 1;
    }
}
echo "copy=", $copy->value, " carriers reached=", $reached, " original=", $cell->value, "\n";
--EXPECT--
agreed=4 of 4
identical=9 of 9
matched=0 values equal=1
copy=7 carriers reached=0 original=42
